course: technology
lesson: 26
title: Escalators and lifts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An escalator is a loop of steps on a chain, pulled round and round by a motor. At the top and bottom the steps flatten out so you can step on and off, then they slide under the floor, turn over and travel back underneath you.
explain: A lift is a box on strong cables that run over a wheel at the top of the building. On the other end hangs a heavy counterweight, so the motor only has to lift the difference, a bit like two children balancing on a seesaw.
explain: In the shopping centre, look down at the sides of the escalator steps: there are bristly brushes there. They are not for cleaning shoes; they gently remind your feet to keep away from the gap, where laces, soft wellies and long scarves can be caught.
explain: All these machines have safety parts you rarely notice: brakes that grip the rails if a cable ever fails, light beams that stop doors closing on you, and alarm buttons that call real people.
fact: An escalator is a loop of steps on a chain pulled by a motor; the steps flatten at the top and bottom, and the handrail moves at the same speed as the steps.
fact: On an escalator, stand still, hold the handrail, keep feet in the middle, tie your laces, keep scarves and long clothes away from the edges, and step off at the top; never sit, play or run on it.
fact: Every escalator has an emergency stop button at the top and the bottom.
fact: The side brushes remind you to keep your feet away from the gap, and the comb plate at the end guides the teeth of the steps safely under the floor.
fact: A lift car is pulled by cables over a pulley with a heavy counterweight on the other side, and strong safety brakes grip the rails if a cable ever fails.
fact: Elisha Otis showed the first lift safety brake in 1854 by having the rope cut while he stood on the raised platform; it did not fall.
fact: Lift doors use sensors, often a curtain of invisible light beams, or a rubber edge, to open again if something is in the way; never stop the doors with hands or feet, wait for the next lift.
fact: The alarm button in a lift rings a bell or calls a help centre; if stuck, stay calm, press it and wait, and never try to climb out. The air will not run out, because lifts are not airtight.
fact: A moving walkway is a flat belt without steps, often at airports; watch for the end, where it warns you.
fact: Skyscraper lifts move several metres a second, and the fastest in the world travel at around 20 metres a second.
fact: Ramps and wheelchair lifts let people who cannot use stairs get in, and many buses kneel down at the stop by lowering their suspension, the springs between the wheels and the body.
fact: A paternoster is an old kind of lift that never stops: open cabins go round in a loop and people step on and off as a cabin slowly passes; a few still work in Germany and the Czech Republic.
fact: A stair lift is a seat on a rail driven by a small motor and battery, so it still works in a power cut; its rail is made to fit the stairs, even round corners. It is not a toy.
exercise: How does an escalator work? | A motor pulls a loop of steps on a chain round and round | Each step has its own little engine hidden underneath it | The steps are pushed up by air from below | It is a slide that the steps are pulled up with a rope
exercise: Why are there brushes along the sides of escalator steps? | To remind your feet to keep away from the gap | To clean and polish people's shoes as they ride up | To make the escalator look nicer | To stop dust getting into the motor
exercise: What is the comb plate at the top of an escalator for? | It guides the teeth of the steps safely under the floor | It combs mud off people's shoes | It is where the escalator stores its spare steps for the night shift | It holds the handrail in place
exercise: Where are an escalator's emergency stop buttons? | At the top and at the bottom | Only in the manager's office upstairs | Hidden under the handrail in the middle | Escalators have no stop button
exercise: Which is the safe way to ride an escalator? | Stand still, hold the handrail and keep feet in the middle | Sit on the step to rest your legs | Run up the side next to the brushes, holding the rubber rail for balance | Walk the wrong way for practice
exercise: What does the counterweight in a lift do? | It balances the car, so the motor only lifts the difference | It stops the lift from going too high | It makes the lift heavier so it can't swing about in the shaft when it moves | It is a spare lift for emergencies
exercise: What happens if a lift cable ever breaks? | Safety brakes grip the rails and stop the car | The lift falls all the way down to the bottom | Air under the lift slows it like a parachute | The lift floats gently up to the top floor
exercise: In what year did Elisha Otis show the first lift safety brake? | 1854 | 1954 | 1754 | 1904
exercise: How do lift doors know not to close on you? | Sensors such as light beams or a rubber edge see something in the way | The lift driver watches on a camera | They close only when nobody is talking | They can't tell, so you must jump through
exercise: What should you do if you are stuck in a lift? | Stay calm, press the alarm and wait for help | Prise the doors open with your fingers and climb out | Jump up and down hard to get the lift moving again | Hold your breath because the air will run out
exercise: Why does a moving walkway warn you near its end? | So you get ready to step off where the belt ends | Because it is about to go much faster at the end | So you know to turn round and walk back the other way | Because the floor after it is wet and slippery
exercise: About how fast do the fastest lifts in the world travel? | About 20 metres a second | Only about 2 metres a minute | About 200 metres a second, faster than a plane | About as fast as a snail on a wall
exercise: Why does a bus tilt down at the stop? | It kneels by lowering its suspension so people can get on easily | The driver brakes too hard | The tyres let out air at each stop | It is leaning to let water run off
exercise: How do you get on a paternoster lift that never stops? | You step on as an open cabin slowly passes | You jump in quickly as it rushes past you | You press a button and it stops for you | You climb a ladder into the moving cabin
exercise: Why does a stair lift still work in a power cut? | It runs on a battery | It is pulled by hand from upstairs | It uses the weight of the person to move | It has a small petrol engine
exercise: A lift can take 8 people at a time. 20 people are waiting. How many trips does it need? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 28
test: «escalator brushes are for polishing ur shoes lol» What are they really for? | They remind your feet to keep away from the gap | Yes, they polish everyone's shoes for free as you ride | They catch all the coins that people drop | They keep the steps warm and dry in winter
test: Your little sister wants to sit on the escalator step with her wellies on. What is right? | Stand her up, hold her hand, and keep her soft boots away from the edges | Fine, sitting is safer than standing | Fine, if she keeps her laces tied | Fine, if the escalator is going down
test: «stuck in the lift!! we're gonna run out of air» What is right? | Lifts aren't airtight, so the air won't run out: press the alarm and wait | Yes, so break the ceiling and climb out | Yes, so shout and use up less air | Open the doors and climb down the shaft
test: In a cartoon, a lift cable snaps and the lift drops 50 floors. What would really happen? | Safety brakes would grip the rails and stop the car | The same, lifts have no brakes | The lift would float like a balloon | The counterweight would pull it back up at once to the very top of the building
test: «doors closing!! I'll just stick my foot in, they always open» What is the best advice? | Don't rely on the sensor; wait for the next lift | Good idea, the sensor never fails, not even once in a hundred years | Use your hand, it's safer than a foot | Kick the doors, they open faster
test: A child's long scarf is caught at the edge of the escalator. What should an adult do at once? | Press the emergency stop button at the top or bottom | Pull the child up the steps faster | Wait for the escalator to reach the top and let the comb plate cut the scarf | Tell a shop assistant after shopping
test: «Gran's stair lift is like a ride, can I go up and down on it?» What is right? | No, it is not a toy; it is for Gran | Yes, it's a fun ride if you hold on tight | Yes, but only if the power is switched off | Only if you go up it sitting backwards
test: A tower's top floor is 400 metres up, and its lift travels 20 metres a second. How long is the ride? | 20 seconds | 4 seconds | 200 seconds | 8 seconds
test: «why does that weird old German lift never stop, is it broken?» What is it? | A paternoster, an old lift whose open cabins go round in a loop | Yes, it's broken and nobody fixed it | It's a fairground ride, not a lift | A lift that stops only at night
test: The escalator is getting near the top. What should you do? | Step off straight away at the top | Stand still on the comb plate to wait for friends | Sit down on the step to slide off at the top | Turn round and walk back down the steps
test: Count them: 3 lifts, each taking 10 people per trip. How many people in one trip of all three? | 30 | 13 | 20 | 33
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the brushes affixed to the sides of an escalator? | They serve to remind passengers to keep their feet clear of the gap between the steps and the side | They are provided for the cleaning of passengers' footwear | They are intended to collect coins and small objects | They serve to warm the steps in winter
exam: Ascertain the consequence which would ensue were the hoisting cable of a lift to fail. | Safety brakes would engage the guide rails and arrest the descent of the car | The car would descend unimpeded to the lowest level | The car would ascend to the highest level | The car would be suspended by the air within the shaft
exam: Identify the course of conduct appropriate to a person confined within a stationary lift. | To remain calm, to operate the alarm button and to await assistance | To force the doors and climb out by way of the shaft | To leap repeatedly in order to restore movement | To restrict breathing on the assumption that the air will be exhausted
exam: It is given that a lift accommodates 8 persons per journey and that 20 persons await it. Determine the minimum number of journeys required. | Three | Two | Four | Twenty-eight
